A||||3||Fformula88||HOT manifolds||||5||
Z||000000||Fformula88||05-15-2000||04:17 PM||||I think my manifolds are running kind of hot.  My ignition wires have those plastic protector bands on them, I assume for the heat.  They are STI wires, and came with them.  Now, the plastic bands are melting.  The wires themselves seem fine.  Could this be my cat thats causing the heat? <P>Also, I have a different engine in my Fiero... its an 86 GT V6 in my 88 Formula.  It was replaced by a previous owner, and I don't know too much about it.  I was wondering if I may be missing any heat shields on the manifolds.  The rear looks like it has one (front of the car) but the trunk side manifold is bare... no heat shield at all.  Should there be one?  Thanks for any and all advice.||||reg||5
Z||000001||N_Man||05-15-2000||06:36 PM||||Sounds like ignition timing to me, too much advance will do it.<P>After that maybe a backed up exaust.  But when I bought my old car (merkur XR4TI) the timing was set for way to much advance and its manifold would glow if it was dark out.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DRH||05-15-2000||06:59 PM||||88 Formula only has one small heat shield on the trunk side manifold. It is between the manifold and the alternator. It doesn't really do anything for the ignition wires.<P>Plastic protector bands??? Are they the accordion looking wire looms similar to what the factory wiring runs thru? If so, most of that stuff is not tough enough to be close to a <B>normal</B> manifold.<P>I'm not sure how you can tell if they are too hot (unless they glow red). Anybody got any ideas??? <P>Assuming the manifolds <B>are</B> overheating, timing and a plugged cat. or muffler like n_man said are good guesses. A severe vacuum leak can also cause hot manifolds. A common leak on Fieros is the tube that runs from the EGR valve to the intake manifold.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fformula88||05-15-2000||09:58 PM||||Yeah, they are those accordian wire looms you are talking about.  Maybe they just cannot take the normal temps, but I thought they were supposed to be there, since they came on the wires.  Maybe not.  They don't glow, so thats a good sign.<P>I am fairly sure its not a vacumn leak.  I have replaced the EGR tube already, the dealer spent 5 hours going over the vacumn lines and repaired any leaks they found (only charged me 1 hour labor...).  They also welded up a couple cracks in the manifolds.  Thanks for everyone's advice... <P>About the heat shield between the manifold and alternator... does it cover the end of the alternator?  I don't have any there.  ||||reg||1
